00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.670
It has a clock coming in

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.670
and and it has a divided clock going

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.350
out. So, the idea of the clock prescaler

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.070
is it takes the master clock, the system

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.790
clock coming in,

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:01.830
and it divides it down by some integer

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:05.430
n, which is a generic parameter, and

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.870
produces an output pulse. This is an

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:09.670
enabling pulse.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.750
Uh that is it goes high for the duration

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.390
of one clock pulse, and it's used to

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.670
select specific clock pulse.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.390
So, this is a general principle in

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.470
designing synchronous circuits. We want

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:20.909
to use one master clock and run

00:06:20.919 --> 00:06:22.750
everything off it. We don't use

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.750
generated or derived clocks because then

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.830
the delays are are difficult to account

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:29.030
for, and the tools don't don't in fact

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.030
don't work so well. But in general, we

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:32.470
want to use one master clock. And what

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.950
we do is we use the clock prescaler to

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.470
select, let's say, every hundredth clock

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.030
or every hundred thousandth clock pulse.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:42.190
The master clock that we use uh with

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:43.790
this hardware, the Basys 3 hardware is

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.470
100 MHz.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:48.310
Um and so, we you know, if if we want to

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.030
sample at a kilohertz, we have to divide

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.590
that down by 100,000.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:54.750
